# Ink parity translation - Markdown.swift → Markdown.cor
# Original: Copyright (c) John Sundell 2019, MIT license
# Translation goal: line-by-line parity, staying close to source structure.
# Friction notes inline with FRICTION: prefix.

module com.ink.api


## A parsed Markdown value, which contains its rendered
## HTML representation, as well as any metadata found at
## the top of the Markdown document.
##
## You create instances of this type by parsing Markdown
## strings using MarkdownParser.
#
# FRICTION #1 (STRUCTURAL): Swift declares `public struct Markdown` with 
# `public var html: String` (mutable through a mutable binding). Under our 
# design, struct forbids state: — data: fields are immutable per-instance. 
# 
# Options for parity:
#   (a) Keep as struct, mark all fields as data:. Public API loses direct
#       field mutation. Users would need `let updated = md.with(html: ...)`.
#   (b) Convert to class. Preserves Swift's mutability semantics but changes
#       value semantics to reference.
#
# Swift's Markdown uses TitleStorage (internal reference-typed box) to enable
# mutation of `title` through a struct's `mutating set`. This pattern is
# unrepresentable in Corvid structs (state: forbidden). So option (b) is 
# closer parity for the semantics; option (a) is closer parity for the type.
#
# For this translation: choosing (a) — keep struct-ness. Note that this
# means the `title` computed-property setter and TitleStorage machinery
# are unrepresentable. Documented below.
#
# This is a first-order finding: Swift's value-type-with-reference-storage
# workaround pattern doesn't survive our struct discipline. Idiomatic 
# translation will need to make a real choice.

public record Markdown:
    data:
        ## The HTML representation of the Markdown, ready to
        ## be rendered in a web browser.
        html: String
        
        ## Any metadata values found at the top of the Markdown
        ## document. See this project's README for more information.
        metadata: Dict<String, String>
        
        # FRICTION #2 (VISIBILITY): Swift's `private let titleHeading` inside
        # a public struct. Corvid has three levels: public, internal, private.
        # `private` in Corvid struct = private to the type. Matches Swift's
        # `private` in struct scope. Clean mapping.
        private titleHeading: Heading?
    
    methods:
        ## The inferred title of the document, from any top-level
        ## heading found when parsing. If the Markdown text contained
        ## two top-level headings, then this property will contain
        ## the first one. Note that this property does not take modifiers
        ## into acccount.
        #
        # FRICTION #3 (COMPUTED PROPERTIES): Swift has computed properties
        # with `{ get { ... } set { ... } }` syntax. Corvid uses methods.
        # For a getter, we have the Computed section (§4.10 Stage 1 built-in),
        # but that's for cached-computed fields. Here Swift is doing:
        #   var title: String? { get { makeTitle() } set { overrideTitle(...) } }
        # Which is a computed getter + computed setter, no storage.
        #
        # Closest parity: `title(self)` getter method. Setter is
        # unrepresentable in a struct (no state to mutate) — see FRICTION #1.
        # 
        # NOTE: In idiomatic version, consider whether Computed section can
        # be extended for setter-hooks, or if class-with-state is required.
        function title(self) -> String?:
            return self.make_title()
        
        # FRICTION #4 (INTERNAL INIT): Swift's `internal init(...)` is 
        # visible within the module. Corvid's default visibility is `internal`,
        # so the modifier can be dropped. But there's a subtler issue:
        # Corvid auto-generates initializers from data: fields. Users don't 
        # write `function init(...)` explicitly for simple field-copying init.
        # 
        # For parity, we write the explicit init. Idiomatic drops it — the
        # generated constructor `Markdown(html: ..., titleHeading: ..., 
        # metadata: ...)` is equivalent.
        # 
        # NOTE: We should explicitly document the auto-generated constructor
        # convention in §6.2 or §6.3. Currently implicit.
        function init(html: String, 
                     titleHeading: Heading?,
                     metadata: Dict<String, String>) -> Markdown:
            # FRICTION #5 (SELF-ASSIGNMENT SYNTAX): Swift's `self.x = y` inside 
            # init is the mutation of a builder-shaped self. Corvid constructors 
            # instantiate via `Type(field: value, ...)` — no self.x assignment.
            # 
            # For parity: rewrite as direct construction.
            return Markdown(
                html: html,
                titleHeading: titleHeading,
                metadata: metadata
            )
    
    # FRICTION #6 (PRIVATE EXTENSION): Swift's `private extension Markdown { ... }`
    # is a syntactic mechanism for grouping private helpers. Corvid has no 
    # extensions in Swift's sense. Sections might be relevant, but sections
    # are semantically categorized (data/state/methods), not just visibility 
    # groups.
    # 
    # Closest parity: put private methods in the same methods: section marked 
    # private. Doesn't preserve the visual grouping but preserves semantics.
    # 
    # NOTE: Some Swift idioms use `extension` purely for organization. Our 
    # design has no direct equivalent. Users who want visual grouping might 
    # use user-defined section aliases, but that changes public metadata.
    
    private methods:
        # FRICTION #7 (NESTED CLASS): Swift declares `final class TitleStorage`
        # nested inside `private extension Markdown`. Purpose: a reference-typed
        # storage box so that mutating a `Markdown` (struct) doesn't require 
        # copying the storage — because the storage is `TitleStorage` which
        # is a class (reference semantics).
        #
        # This is a Swift-specific value-type-workaround pattern. In Corvid:
        #   - Nested type declarations are supported syntactically.
        #   - But the SEMANTIC of "reference-storage-in-value-type-for-mutability"
        #     doesn't apply because struct methods can't mutate anyway.
        #
        # For parity: this whole TitleStorage pattern is UNREPRESENTABLE in an
        # Corvid struct. In an Corvid CLASS, it would be unnecessary (state: fields
        # can mutate directly).
        #
        # Documenting the unrepresentable-ness:
        # 
        # @unrepresentable
        # nested class TitleStorage:
        #     state:
        #         title: String?
        
        # FRICTION #8 (MUTATING METHODS): Swift's `mutating func overrideTitle`
        # marks a method that mutates self. Corvid struct methods cannot mutate.
        # class methods can mutate through state: fields.
        # 
        # For parity in a struct: unrepresentable. Note it.
        # 
        # @unrepresentable_in_struct
        # function override_title(self, title: String?):
        #     # Swift body:
        #     #   let storage = TitleStorage()
        #     #   storage.title = title
        #     #   titleStorage = storage
        #     pass
        
        function make_title(self) -> String?:
            # FRICTION #9 (IF-LET): Swift's `if let stored = titleStorage.title { ... }`
            # binds an optional to a non-optional in a scope. 
            # 
            # Corvid has similar pattern via `if let` (assuming — need to verify §3
            # actually specifies this).
            # 
            # NOTE: Grammar sketch §10 doesn't explicitly show if-let. Check §3.
            # If not present, this is a spec gap for parity translations.
            
            # FRICTION #10 (TITLESTORAGE MISSING): The original returns 
            # `titleStorage.title` after caching. Without TitleStorage 
            # representable, we can't cache; must recompute every time.
            # This changes performance semantics but not correctness.
            
            # Parity, without caching (since TitleStorage is unrepresentable):
            return self.titleHeading?.plain_text()
            
            # FRICTION #11 (OPTIONAL CHAINING): `titleHeading?.plainText()` in 
            # Swift. Same shape in Corvid: `self.titleHeading?.plain_text()`. 
            # Should work. Verify against §3.
